# Attenuation design

How a Fearless main gets less than the full authority of `System`,
and how a main runs another main of the same project in a new process with the same attenuators or more.

The algorithms of this design specify the behaviour, not the implementation: an implementation may take the steps of an algorithm in another order, or in another way, when no person and no program can tell the difference, also after a crash.

### Fearless facts

`System` is the root object capability: every observation of the world goes through a method of `System` or of a capability `System` gives (`CapTry`, `CapOutput`, `CapInputCursor`, `CapAssetRead`, `CapDownload`, `CapDataRead`, `CapDataEdit`, `CapManager`, `CapProcess`, `CapLogReader`, `CapGui`, `CapIql`, `CapRevocationRoot`).
`System` and every capability it gives are not sealed: Fearless code can implement them.
A main is `Main: CaptureFree { .main(sys: mut System): Void }`; the runtime gives it a `System` built from the one real `System` of the process (see Deterministic reconstruction).
Observable Determinism: a method with no mut parameters, receiver included, returns structurally equivalent results for structurally equivalent inputs; non termination and deterministic errors are results.
A non deterministic error is caught only by a `CapTry`, and a `CapTry` comes only from a `System`.
A type implementing `CaptureFree` captures nothing. A type implementing `CaptureFree` with no abstract methods and zero generic parameters has one instance, and its type name denotes that instance: the type is 'summonable'. A top level type captures nothing by construction; a type declared inside a method captures nothing only when it implements `CaptureFree`.
`Info` is the one value with a text form that parses back: `Info.str` is JSON text, `Infos.fromStr` reads it.

### Terms

From the object capability literature, used here with one meaning each:
- 'object capability': an object that can do a privileged thing; holding a reference to it is the permission to do that thing. `System` and every capability it gives are object capabilities.
- 'authority': what a program can do to the world. A capability carries authority; a `mut System` carries all of it.
- 'attenuation': deriving from a capability a capability with no more authority. An 'attenuator' of a capability type `C` is a function from a `mut C` to a `mut C` with no more authority. Not: limiter, watchdog, restriction, filter.
- 'revoke': to take the authority away from a capability already given. A capability 'revoked' is one with no authority: every `mut` method of it throws, except `.iso`, which gives a revoked copy, and `.close`.
- 'reconstruction': building in a new process the attenuated `System` of another process from the names of its attenuators, instead of transferring any object.

## Current design

### Attenuators

```
Attenuator[C]: CaptureFree, F[mut C, mut C]{}
SystemAttenuator: Attenuator[System]{}
```
An attenuator of `System` receives a `mut System` and gives a `mut System` whose methods reach the world only through the received one.
An attenuator declared inside a method body has a type name too: the name in the source, or the name the inference chooses for an anonymous attenuator.
The attenuators of a process are summonable: the process finds each of them by its type name.
An attenuator chooses, for each method of the `System` it gives, whether the errors of that method are deterministic or non deterministic.

Example:
```
NoInput: SystemAttenuator { u -> mut ErrorSystem{'s
  .try -> u.try;
  .out -> u.out;
  .assetRead -> u.assetRead;
  //.inputCursor, .download, .dataRead, .dataEdit, .locked omitted: they throw the non deterministic error of ErrorSystem
  //.manager must be omitted near every time since it can give near absolute power back
  .process -> u.process.with NoInput;
  .readLog l -> u.readLog l;
  .gui -> u.gui;
  .revocation -> u.revocation;
  .randomSeed -> u.randomSeed;
  .iso -> NoInput#(u.iso.close);
  .close -> s;
  }}
```
Attenuators compose by application: in `NoGui#(NoInput#(sys))` the `System` that `NoGui` receives is the `System` that `NoInput` gives, so no attenuator recovers authority that an inner attenuator took away.
`NoGui#(NoInput#(sys))` and `NoInput#(NoGui#(sys))` give the same authority; for other attenuators the order of application can change the result.

### ErrorSystem, Revocation and RevokableSystem

```
ErrorSystem: System { ...every method throws...; .iso -> ErrorSystem; .close -> this; }
Revocation: ToIso[Revocation]{ mut #: Void; }
CapRevocationRoot: Revocation, ToIso[CapRevocationRoot]{ mut .start: Void; mut .revoke: Void; }
System: ... { ...; mut .revocation: mut CapRevocationRoot; }
RevokableSystem: { #(r: mut Revocation, sys: mut System): mut System -> {
  .try->Block#(r#,RevokableTry#(r,sys.try));
  ...
  .iso->RevokableSystem#(r.iso.close,sys.iso.close);
  } }
```
`ErrorSystem` is a `System` with no authority: every method throws the non deterministic error `System.<method> is revoked.`, `.iso` gives another `ErrorSystem` and `.close` gives itself.

```
Flag: { .notStarted | .started | .revoked }
sys.revocation  gives a new root r, with a new flag(r) = .notStarted
r.iso           gives a copy r' with flag(r') = flag(r): the same flag, not a copy of it
r#              flag(r) = .started    -> returns
                flag(r) = .notStarted -> non deterministic error `Revocation.start was not called yet.`
                flag(r) = .revoked    -> non deterministic error `Revocation.revoke was called.`
r.start         flag(r) = .notStarted -> flag(r) := .started
                otherwise             -> non deterministic error `Revocation.start was called twice.`
r.revoke        flag(r) = .started    -> flag(r) := .revoked
                flag(r) = .notStarted -> non deterministic error `Revocation.revoke was called before .start.`
                flag(r) = .revoked    -> returns
```
The errors of `.start` and `.revoke` are non deterministic: the holder of a copy has no way to know the flag.
A `Revocation` holds no value and has no `read` method: the copies share the flag and nothing else, so a copy promoted to `imm` is inert.

`RevokableSystem#(r,sys)` wraps `sys` by calling `r#` at every delegation of a `mut` method, and returning revocable versions of all the other capabilities, transitively: one wrapper type per capability type.
The wrapper of `CapGui` stops at `.run`: the `Frame` and widgets `.run` gives are not wrapped.
A `read` method of a wrapper delegates without calling `r#`: only `mut` methods are object capability methods.
`.iso` does not check the flag: the copy it gives checks the flag at every call, so a revoked `System` gives copies that are revoked too.

Implementation details:
- `Revocation` is native: the copies `r.iso` gives share one flag;
- copies of one root reach parallel flow stages as iso elements or as the iso `ctx` of a stage, so `#`, `.start` and `.revoke` run at the same time on one flag: the flag is one volatile field;
- each wrapper type of `RevokableSystem` is as long as the capability type it wraps.

### Deterministic reconstruction

Every process running a main gives the main its `System` as follows:
```
Block#
  .let r= {realSystem.revocation}
  .let s= {RevokableSystem#(r,realSystem)}
  .let attenuated= {An#(...(A1#(s)))}
  .do{ r.start }
  .return{ main.main(attenuated) }
```
`A1`...`An` are the attenuators of the process, in order.
While the attenuators run, `flag(r) = .notStarted`: a call on `s` other than `.iso` and `.close` throws a non deterministic error, and that error ends the process.
The `System` the attenuators build is structurally equivalent in every process applying the same names in the same order.

### Processes

```
System: ... { mut .process: mut CapProcess; }
CapProcess: ToIso[CapProcess], WidenTo[CapProcess]{
  mut .run(main: Main, attenuators: List[SystemAttenuator]): Void;
  mut .with(attenuator: SystemAttenuator): mut CapProcess;
  }
```
`sys.process.run(SecondMain, as)`, with `as` the attenuators `A1`...`An`, behaves as `SecondMain.main(An#(...A1#(sys)))`, except that `SecondMain` runs in a new process of this program, and `.run` returns when that process ends.
The new process applies the attenuators of `.with`, in their order, then `attenuators`, in their order, and no other attenuator.
The rule above gives the following property; the property is not an operation of any program. With s a `System`, and A1..An the attenuators that built s:
```
(forall i in 1..n. Ai gives .process as u.process.with Ai)  =>  every process that s.process starts has at most the authority of s
```

The new process starts as this one started: same first input file, same data projects, same manager, same desk id (see Desk ids and icons in ControllersDesign), same working folder; it prints to the output of this process. A file dropped on a window of a process arrives on the `CapInputCursor` of that process.
The Output has no mark for a process that `CapProcess` starts (see Run, Compile and Check in ControllersDesign).
`.run` and `.start` accept every summonable `Main` and every summonable `InfoMain` of every package of the project; the new process finds the main or the `InfoMain` by its type name, as it finds an attenuator.
A process that can not start, or that ends before its main ends (a JVM error, a kill), is a non deterministic error of `.run`.
The end of a process also ends every process it started.

Implementation details:
- the 'parent lifeline' of a process is its standard input: a process started with `-Dfearless.parentLifeline=stdin` ends when its standard input reaches its end, which the end of its parent causes; a new process inherits the property with the input arguments;
- the attenuators of a process are the system property `fearlessAttenuators`,
the qualified type names separated by commas; no property means no attenuator;
- the `Main.java` of each package summons each attenuator by reflection from its qualified name;
- the new JVM is the `java` of `java.home` with the input arguments of this JVM, the class path of this JVM, the `Main` class of the package of the main (`_CapProcess` of `rt` for an `InfoMain`) and the name of the main; the properties `fearlessAttenuators`, `fearlessProcess.resultMarker` and `fearless.endMarker` are not inherited;
- for an `InfoMain`, the first line of the standard input of the new process is the JSON text of `arg`, and the parent lifeline is the rest of the standard input;
- the new process uses its standard output and error as the calling process does; the calling process reads them and pushes them to its own standard output and error, as the manager reads the output of the main it started;
- the result travels on the standard error of the new process, after everything else it printed: a marker line the calling process chose and passed as `fearlessProcess.resultMarker`, then `ok` (followed by the JSON text of the result of an `InfoMain`), or `det` or `nondet` followed by the JSON text of the `Info`, with the stack frames of the new process as `CapTry` records them;
- the calling process forwards the error output up to the marker, parses what follows after the new process ended, and rethrows the `Info` with the same kind; a `CapTry` of the calling process appends its own frames to the frames of the new process;
- a process started with a marker prints its uncaught `Info` only in the marker form.

### Manager

The `.attenuators` of the `.manifest` of `<pkg>.json` (see The Coordinator in CoordinatorDesign) are the `SystemAttenuator` types of the package with no abstract method.
The attenuators of `base` are attenuators of every project (`.packages` and `._mergedManifest` in The Coordinator in CoordinatorDesign).
The manager starts each main with the attenuators that `attenuators` of `projects.info` gives the main (see Registered projects in ControllersDesign), as the `.attenuators` of `Launch` (see The Coordinator in CoordinatorDesign).

The project area shows the attenuators in the Attenuators collapsible area (see Project area in ControllersDesign).

